title: $:/plugins/nikorion/base-fields/language/en-GB/readme
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

!! Overview

Adds seven everyday fields to the tiddler edit template:

|!Field |!Row |!Control |!Stored value |
|`tools` |Tags |list field, edited like tags |a list of titles |
|`project` |Tags |single value, typed or picked |free text |
|`role` |Type |controlled vocabulary, single value |a bare slug (`concept`, `guide`, …) |
|`maturity` |Type |controlled vocabulary, single value, //hidden on register and task roles// |a bare slug (`stub`, `stable`, …) |
|`status` |Type |controlled vocabulary, single value, //shown on task, tasklist, hub and routine roles only// |a bare slug (`todo`, `doing`, …) |
|`lifecycle` |Type |controlled vocabulary, single value, empty means active |a bare slug (`obsolete`, `archived`) |
|`needs` |Below Type |controlled vocabulary, //list// (one checkbox per value), hidden on register and task roles |a list of bare slugs (`verify refactor`) |

The added controls sit on rows of their own, each right under the core row it extends: ''Tools'' and ''Project'' share the row under the tags box, ''Role'', ''Maturity'', ''Status'' and ''Lifecycle'' the one under the type control, and ''Needs'' the one below that. No core tiddler is overridden and no core row is touched.

A row packs its blocks to the left at their natural width rather than spreading them out, so that a block's own parts stay together — the delete button sits next to the control it clears rather than at the far end of a stretched row. Too narrow to hold them all, a row wraps.

Tools and Project carry no visible label: their placeholder says what they are for, which spares the width.

The tools and tags pickers are strictly independent: the tools dropdown offers the values already used in `tools`, never the wiki's tags, and vice versa. Tool pills are rendered like tag pills, per-value colour cascade included, but fall back to an orange of their own rather than the palette's tag colour, so the two kinds of pill stay told apart on a tiddler carrying both. `$:/config/nikorion/base-fields/tools/Colour` holds that fallback.

''Status'' says how far along a piece of work is, so it only appears where that means something — on a tiddler whose role is `task`, `tasklist`, `hub` or `routine`. Which roles those are is set on the vocabulary itself, in the `visible-filter` field of `vocab/status`, so that the rule sits next to the values it governs.

A control that is only there because its field still holds a value the role no longer accounts for (say `needs` after the role changed to `task`) is framed in red, and its tooltip names the role and says to clear the field (//The tasks do not use this field — clear it//).

Each control carries the same clear button the core puts on Type, always visible. An empty Nature or Status shows a greyed hint in place of a value — a `<select>` has no placeholder attribute, so its blank first option plays that part.

The seven fields are hidden from the list of fields at the bottom of the editor, through the core's own `$:/config/EditTemplateFields/Visibility/` mechanism: editing them twice on one form would be one place too many. Setting the corresponding tiddler to `show` brings any of them back.

!! Controlled vocabularies

A //slug// is the short, lowercase, unpunctuated identifier that stands for a value — `snippet`, `stub`. The word comes from the printing trade: a slug was a line of text cast in a single piece of metal, and newsrooms came to call the short working name written on a story in production its slug. Web publishing kept it for the identifier that stands for something in a URL, or here in a field.

Only the slug is ever written to the field. Icons and labels are display-only, resolved at render time, so a wiki stays readable and filterable in any language:

* the //allowed values and their order// live in the `list` field of `vocab/role`, `vocab/maturity`, `vocab/status`, `vocab/lifecycle` and `vocab/needs`
* the //icons// live in `vocab/icons.multids`, keyed `<field>/<value>`
* the //labels// live in `language/<lang>/vocab.multids`, keyed `Vocab/<field>/<value>`

A value shows the same way everywhere, dropdowns included: its icon and its label in the wiki's language — `Snippet` in English, `Extrait` in French. The slug never appears; it is an internal identifier, not something to read. Each value can also carry a one-line description, shown as a tooltip on hover, with the stored value on a second line: the `Vocab/<field>/<value>/Hint` strings, optional.

The //Vocabularies// tab of this plugin is the reference list of the five fields and their values: for each one, its icon and label, the slug exactly as it is stored, and what it means. None of it is written by hand — the page is regenerated on every render from the vocabularies themselves, so it always gives the wiki's current state rather than its original one: a value added, removed or reordered shows up there at once, while this readme stays as it is. That is where to look for what a field accepts today, not here, and it is the page to point a reader at.

Adding, removing or reordering a value is done in the plugin source, not through the UI — there is no vocabulary editor, on purpose: a value added through the interface could not carry its translations. To add one, append its slug to the relevant `list` field, then add its icon and its label in each language. A value with no label falls back to its own slug; a value with no icon simply renders without one. The `role` values are further split into groups, which the dropdown shows as greyed headings above the values they gather: name an added value in one of the `group-<slug>` fields, or it is offered on its own, before the first heading.

Shipped out of the box — the starting point, before anything a wiki adds or removes of its own; the //Vocabularies// tab is what says where a given wiki stands now:

* `role` — hub; //Knowledge//: concept, ref, source; //Procedure//: guide, routine; //Reuse//: snippet, template; //Register//: decision, log; //Tracking//: task, tasklist
* `maturity` — stub, rough, stable
* `status` — considering, todo, doing, paused, done, dropped
* `lifecycle` — obsolete, archived (empty means active)
* `needs` — verify, refactor, split (a list: several can apply at once)

Helpers are exposed globally, for use in view templates and lists.

`bf-vocab-item` (icon + label) is a //procedure//. A procedure called as `<<name arg>>` receives its arguments as literal text, so a field's value has to be passed through a transclusion rather than a macro call:

```
<$transclude $variable="bf-vocab-item" field="role" value={{!!role}}/>
```

`bf-vocab-values`, `bf-vocab-label`, `bf-vocab-icon` and `bf-vocab-hint` are //functions//, and give the pieces separately. Call them through the `function` operator, which does bind their parameters:

```
<$let slug={{!!role}}>{{{ [function[bf-vocab-label],[role],<slug>] }}}</$let>
```

!! How it works

Everything is an edit-template section: two tiddlers tagged `$:/tags/EditTemplate`, placed with `list-after`, plus a stylesheet, some global procedures and the vocabularies. Nothing overrides a core tiddler, so a TiddlyWiki upgrade carries no risk of silently reverting part of the editor, and nothing collides with another plugin that touches the same rows.

Where each row lands is decided by the `list-after` of its section alone; the stylesheet only lays out what is inside a row. Rows therefore stay independent of one another: growing the tags box by a line pushes the row below down, and moves nothing sideways. The tools box is sized by what it holds rather than by its row, so Project follows straight after it instead of sitting at the far edge; its pills flow beside its input and wrap inside it just as the core's tags do, and Project drops to the line below once they fill the width.

Two details worth knowing:

# the tools and project inputs drive the core `keyboard-driven-input` macro directly rather than the `tag-picker` macro, which hardcodes the tags placeholder and reuses the tag input state the edit template defines once for the whole form — sharing it would let a half-typed tool name be added as a //tag// by the save keyboard shortcut
# their completion filters live on `search-filters`, read through `configTiddlerFilter` and `firstSearchFilterField`

!! Requirements

TiddlyWiki >= 5.3.0. No other dependency.

